<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zograf Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zografou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zograf Monastery is the Bulgarian monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, traditionally founded in the late 10th century and dedicated to Saint George. Its name, 'the painter', comes from a legend of an icon that painted itself. It has long been a centre of Bulgarian culture: there in 1762 the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paisius-of-hilendar">Paisius of Hilendar</a> completed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonic-bulgarian-history">Slavonic-Bulgarian History</a>, which helped begin the Bulgarian national revival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zograf-monastery.jpg" alt="Zograf Monastery" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georgid · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zograf_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
